Z||000023||Richard Parnell||05-17-2000||08:27 PM||||I think American car manufacturers would tap into a gold mine if they started producing technologically updated versions of old favorites, 55-58 Chevy, 55 Ford, Studebaker Hawk, 49-50 Mercury coupe, and so on.  Lightweight new V8 engines and cool looking.  I think it's a winner.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Raydar||05-18-2000||11:29 AM||||This is off on a slight tangent, but bear with me...<BR>Nissan is (was?) refurbishing old 240Z's, and selling them as "restored" through dealers. (Haven't heard anything about this for a while. Possible they encountered some problems.) <BR>Makes me wonder why GM -or anybody else- couldn't "restore" their own products. I wonder how much of the original car would have to remain. Body shell? Floorpan?  Frame rails? Just the VIN plate? <BR>Reminds me of the story of the woodcutter who had an axe that was handed down from his grandfather. The handle had been replaced for times, and the head had been replaced twice, but it was still a great axe!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
